\section{Methods and Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

\subsection{Architectures and Benchmark}
\label{sec:setup_overview}

We study five VLA architectures spanning three vision connector families (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}).

\textbf{Linear projection.} $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B parameters, 256 tokens) maps SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} patches through a linear layer into a PaliGemma backbone and generates actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M parameters, 50 tokens) augments its linear projection with a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart}; self-attention in these layers introduces cross-token mixing that makes the effective pipeline more complex than the nominal ``linear projection'' label suggests.

\textbf{MLP projector.} OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B parameters, 256 tokens) fuses DINOv2~\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP encoders through a three-layer MLP (GELU activations) that maps each patch independently into a Llama-2 backbone. OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} shares this architecture with an optimized fine-tuning action head; we include it for attention entropy comparison only.

\textbf{Spatial folding.} SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, 128 tokens from two cameras) encodes each camera image with SigLIP into 1024 patches, then applies a pixel-shuffle connector that folds each $4{\times}4$ block into one token by channel concatenation (64 per camera). Each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches; removing one eliminates an entire $4{\times}4$ receptive field. The tokens enter a Qwen2-0.5B backbone~\cite{yang2024qwen2} with flow matching action generation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector comparison. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds $4{\times}4$ patches into single tokens (128 total, spatially entangled). OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently (256 tokens, per-token independence).}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

We evaluate on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks organized into three suites: Object (10 tasks), Spatial (10 tasks), and Goal (10 tasks). Each suite uses 50 episodes per task (500 total), evaluated by closed-loop Success Rate (SR) with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals. Full fine-tuning baselines on Object: SmolVLA 44.8\%, OpenVLA 74.0\%, X-VLA 99.2\%.

\subsection{SmolVLA Pruning Ablation}
\label{sec:smolvla_ablation_setup}

We train a lightweight ACIS module (3.46M parameters, 0.77\% of SmolVLA) to predict per-token importance scores with VLA weights frozen. The ablation varies scoring (learned ACIS vs.\ random), retention ratio ($k \in \{0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging via ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome}), yielding seven configurations.

\subsection{Cross-Model Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:openvla_eval_setup}

Cross-model evaluation uses two complementary protocols. \emph{Offline diagnostic analysis} quantifies token importance structure: for each model, we compute the L2 norm of every vision token, rank tokens by norm, and zero out the bottom $(1{-}k)$ fraction. Offline single-step action loss is recorded across retention ratios. This protocol isolates token heterogeneity because L2 norm ranking preserves the most informative tokens by construction. $\pi_0$ is evaluated at $k \in \{0.1, 0.5\}$ (normalized L1 action loss). OFT provides diagnostic measurements (attention entropy, token importance CV) without pruning evaluation.

\emph{Closed-loop evaluation} tests robustness on LIBERO-Object under two pruning strategies that differ in difficulty. OpenVLA uses random token removal (uniform random selection, physical removal of unselected tokens) at $k \in \{0.30, 0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\}$, retaining 30\%--95\% of 256 vision tokens. Random removal provides a conservative lower bound because it does not preserve the most informative tokens. X-VLA uses L2 norm-based removal (rank by token norm, physically remove lowest-norm tokens) at $k \in \{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$. Importance-based removal is a more favorable test because it preferentially retains high-norm tokens. The fact that OpenVLA degrades smoothly under the harder test (random) while X-VLA exhibits a threshold at $k{=}0.7$ under the easier test (importance-based) strengthens the architecture-dependent conclusion. SmolVLA is evaluated under both strategies and multiple mechanisms (zero-masking, true removal, token merging; Section~\ref{sec:smolvla_ablation_setup}), all producing 0\% SR.

Two metrics characterize each model's vision token structure from a single forward pass on task-relevant frames. \emph{Attention entropy ratio}: normalized Shannon entropy of attention weights over vision tokens, where low entropy indicates concentrated attention on a few tokens. \emph{Token importance CV}: coefficient of variation of L2 norms across vision tokens, where high CV signals heterogeneous importance distribution.

\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

We insert a cross-attention pooling module (22.2M parameters, $\sim$5\% of SmolVLA) between the pixel-shuffle connector and LLM backbone. $M$ learned queries attend over all 128 source tokens, producing $M$ compressed representations. We evaluate $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$ ($M{=}128$ is a passthrough control). A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) serves as an architecture control. Training details are in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}.

