\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We evaluate token pruning across five VLA models spanning three connector families on LIBERO-Object~\cite{liu2023libero} (10 tasks, 50 episodes each, 500 total). All success rates include Wilson 95\% confidence intervals for single-seed results and mean $\pm$ standard deviation for multi-seed results.

\subsection{Taxonomy Overview}
\label{sec:taxonomy_overview}

Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy} summarizes the five models and their pruning responses. Effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity, rather than the nominal connector label, determines pruning tolerance: pure linear projection ($\pi_0$) is immune, linear projection + deep encoder (X-VLA) is threshold-graded, MLP degrades smoothly, and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically. Token importance CV tracks this ordering continuously (0.038, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314) across a 16$\times$ parameter range (450M--7B). A controlled comparison between $\pi_0$ and OpenVLA---both using 256 SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} tokens, differing only in connector---yields a 63\,pp gap at $k{=}0.1$, isolating the pipeline as causal (Section~\ref{sec:linear_results}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-model connector taxonomy. $^\dagger\pi_0$ reports offline normalized L1 action loss (pending closed-loop evaluation). $^\ddagger$X-VLA uses a linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder; the effective pipeline is not purely linear. $^\S$OFT reports attention entropy and CV only (no pruning evaluation); both metrics match standard OpenVLA, confirming the connector determines token importance structure.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\begin{tabular}{llcrll}
\toprule
Model & Connector & Params & Tokens & Representative Result & Class \\
\midrule
$\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} & Linear proj & 3.3B & 256 & $k{=}0.1$: $\mathcal{L}_1{=}0.976$ ($+$2.4\%)$^\dagger$ & Immune (offline) \\
X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} & Linear proj$^\ddagger$ & 880M & 50 & $k{=}0.9$: $97.8{\pm}0.4$, $k{=}0.85$: $67.8$ & Thresh.-graded \\
OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} & 3-layer MLP & 7B & 256 & $k{=}0.5$: 37.4\% SR ($-$49\%) & Smooth graded \\
OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} & 3-layer MLP & 7B & 256 & CV $0.219 \approx 0.215$; Ent.\ $0.667 \approx 0.661$$^\S$ & Smooth graded \\
SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} & Pixel-shuffle & 450M & 128 & $k{=}0.95$: 0\% SR & Catastrophic cliff \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Pixel-Shuffle: Catastrophic Cliff-Edge}
\label{sec:smolvla_results}

The SmolVLA baseline (bypass mode, all 128 vision tokens) achieves 44.8\% SR (224/500, CI [40.5\%, 49.2\%]).\footnote{The official SmolVLA result is 87.3\% SR with batch size 64 on 8 GPUs. Our reproduction uses a smaller batch due to compute constraints.} Every pruning configuration produces 0\% SR across seven configurations varying scoring (learned ACIS vs.\ random), retention ratio ($k \in \{0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging); full ablation in Table~\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}, Appendix~\ref{app:smolvla_ablation}. The official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2\% SR) exhibits identical failure, confirming the cliff-edge is architectural. The pattern generalizes across LIBERO suites (Spatial: 0.6\%, Goal: 0\%).

\subsection{MLP Projector: Graded Degradation}
\label{sec:openvla_results}

Under random token removal, OpenVLA degrades gracefully across all retention ratios: 69.4\% SR at $k{=}0.95$ ($-4.6$pp from the 74.0\% baseline), 62.0\% at $k{=}0.90$, 49.0\% at $k{=}0.70$, 37.4\% at $k{=}0.50$, and 20.6\% at $k{=}0.30$ (Figure~\ref{fig:results_combined}a; full results with CIs in Table~\ref{tab:openvla_full}, Appendix).\footnote{A second random seed confirms the graded pattern: 62.2\% at $k{=}0.9$ ($+$0.2pp), 52.6\% at $k{=}0.7$ ($+$3.6pp), 35.6\% at $k{=}0.5$ ($-$1.8pp). All within 4\,pp of the primary seed, confirming the degradation curve is reproducible. Per-seed details in Table~\ref{tab:openvla_multiseed}, Appendix~\ref{app:openvla_multiseed}.} At $k{=}0.30$, two tasks (cream\_cheese, butter) reach 0\% SR, but eight retain non-zero performance: degradation is graded, not cliff-edge. Per-task breakdowns are in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}. Random token removal provides a conservative lower bound on architectural resilience; learned scoring methods would likely yield better retention.

OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} retains OpenVLA's MLP connector but replaces the action head; its attention entropy (0.667) matches standard OpenVLA (0.661), confirming the connector, not the action head, determines token importance structure. EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} corroborates the graded pattern: CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact} (MLP projector) retains 74.2\% SR under 78\% pruning.

To control for scoring method in cross-architecture comparisons, we evaluate L2 norm-based selection on OpenVLA. At moderate-to-aggressive retention ($k \leq 0.7$), L2 performs within 2--4\,pp of random removal (Table~\ref{tab:openvla_l2_vs_random}, Appendix~\ref{app:l2_vs_random}), confirming that the cross-architecture comparison is not confounded by the choice of scoring criterion. At $k{=}0.9$, L2 outperforms random by 8.5\,pp (70.6\% vs.\ 62.0\%, reproducible across two random seeds), consistent with the weak but non-zero importance gradient predicted by CV$\,{=}\,$0.215: the signal is exploitable only when few tokens are removed.

\subsection{Linear Projection: From Immune to Threshold-Graded}
\label{sec:linear_results}

$\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B, 256 tokens, linear projection) is evaluated via offline normalized L1 action loss.\footnote{Offline action loss measures single-step prediction quality; closed-loop evaluation may reveal additional degradation. X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop gap (Section~\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}) underscores the need for live verification.} At $k{=}0.1$, the loss is 0.976---a 2.4\% \emph{improvement} over the unpruned baseline. At $k{=}0.5$ the loss remains 0.983.

X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, 50 tokens) routes vision tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} before the LLM. Under L2 norm-based removal, the 99.2\% baseline is maintained at $k{=}0.95$ ($99.1 \pm 0.2$\%, 3 seeds) and $k{=}0.9$ ($97.8 \pm 0.4$\%). A sharp cliff appears at $k{=}0.85$ (67.8\%, $-30$\,pp, 2 seeds), with non-monotonic recovery at $k{=}0.80$ (88.6\%), before resuming degradation at $k{=}0.7$ ($57.2 \pm 1.6$\%, 3 seeds), $43.7 \pm 1.0$\% at $k{=}0.5$, $22.3 \pm 1.1$\% at $k{=}0.3$, $4.7 \pm 0.6$\% at $k{=}0.2$, and near-zero at $k{=}0.1$. The cliff onset lies at $k{=}0.85$ (retaining 42 of 50 BART-encoded tokens), sharper than the previously reported $k{=}0.7$ threshold. The non-monotonic dip at $k{=}0.85$ relative to $k{=}0.80$ is reproducible across 2 seeds and task-specific: four tasks lose $\sim$50\,pp each while the remaining six are unaffected (Table~\ref{tab:xvla_cliff_pertask}, Appendix~\ref{app:xvla_cliff}). The cliff is reproducible across 3 seeds at $k{=}0.7$ (std 1.6pp) despite extreme per-task variance. That this threshold appears under importance-based removal (the more favorable test) while OpenVLA degrades smoothly under random removal (the harder test) strengthens the architecture-dependent conclusion. The $\pi_0$--X-VLA contrast shows the connector label alone is insufficient: effective pipeline complexity determines pruning tolerance.

\subsection{VTR: Re-Encoding Overcomes Cliff-Edge}
\label{sec:smart_compress_results}

To test whether learned re-encoding can overcome the pixel-shuffle cliff-edge, we insert a 22.2M-parameter cross-attention module (Vision Token Re-encoder, VTR) between the vision connector and the LLM backbone (details in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}). VTR compresses 128 input tokens to $M$ output tokens. Both VTR and the baseline train from the same SmolVLA-Base checkpoint for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters. An extended training control (same procedure without the compressor) achieves 45.0\% SR ($p > 0.9$ vs.\ the 44.8\% baseline), which confirms the gain is architectural, not from additional optimization steps.\footnote{The extended baseline also collapses to 0\% SR under pruning ($k{=}0.50$, random, true removal), which confirms that additional training does not mitigate the pixel-shuffle vulnerability.}

